** A practical build order you can follow **
If you want a concrete sequence:

Core & skeleton:

main.py, core/config.py, core/logging.py, db/session.py, Alembic.

Auth & RBAC:

models/user.py, models/role.py, auth/jwt.py, core/security.py, core/rbac.py, services/auth_service.py, routers/auth_router.py.

Core domain models & schemas:

News, events, officers, directors, programs, documents, photos, prayers, voting, market, etc.

Public router:

All /public/* endpoints (read-only).

Member router:

Profile, events, programs, documents, voting, market.

Admin routers (priority first):

Content, events, members, governance; then media, communications, market, webmaster.

Media router:

File serving and thumbnails.

Background tasks & audit:

Celery app, email/media/voting/audit tasks, audit log integration.

You can absolutely iterate feature-by-feature, but try to keep each feature slice vertical: models → schemas → services → router. That way, every slice is testable in isolation (even if you’re skipping formal tests for now).













Reusable CRUD service template

It gives you a predictable pattern:

>  router → service → model → db

Once you adopt this pattern, every feature becomes easy to implement.


From here, the rest of the backend is feature implementation, not architecture.

Each feature follows the same pattern:

1. Model
2. Schema (Pydantic)
3. Service (using BaseCRUDService)
4. Router (using CRUD router template)
5. Optional custom endpoints
6. Optional admin-only routes

You already have the templates for #2–#4.

So building each feature becomes a repeatable, predictable workflow.